    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.64599/yjnh8881",
    "abstract": "This study analyzes the meteorological and human-biometeorological conditions of Mravaldzali (Oni Municipality, Georgia) and their implications for thermal comfort and tourism suitability. Using hourly data from 2015\u20132025, key meteorological variables were evaluated across multiple temporal scales, and thermal conditions were assessed with UTCI and PET. Tourism-relevant climate characteristics were synthesized through the Climate\u2013Tourism Information Scheme (CTIS). Findings reveal pronounced seasonal differentiation: persistent cold stress from late autumn to early spring reduces the suitability for thermally comfort-dependent outdoor activities, while summer (June\u2013September) shows the highest frequency of thermally neutral conditions and minimal heat stress. Precipitation is generally low and dominated by light rainfall, wind speeds remain mostly moderate, and fog \u2014especially in colder and transitional seasons\u2014 represents a relevant atmospheric condition influencing visibility and accessibility, while also contributing to distinctive scenic features such as mist-filled valleys and \u201csea of clouds\u201d that enhance the landscape character of mountain environments. The study highlights the need for climate-sensitive assessments based on actual tourism exposure hours and recommends establishing local meteorological monitoring and applying higher temporal resolution analyses to better capture microclimatic variability in mountainous environments.",
    "field": "Tourism climatology",
    "problem": "Generic climate assessments are inadequate for tourism planning in complex mountain environments because they fail to capture significant microclimatic variability and specific human-centered thermal comfort conditions, which are crucial for determining a destination's suitability and supporting sustainable development.",
    "method": "This study proposes an integrated method using hourly meteorological data, biometeorological indices (PET, UTCI), and the Climate-Tourism Information Scheme (CTIS) to assess the thermal comfort and tourism suitability of a mountainous region in Georgia.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze the meteorological and human-biometeorological conditions of Mravaldzali, Georgia, to assess their implications for thermal comfort and tourism suitability using indices such as PET, UTCI, and the Climate-Tourism Information Scheme.",
